Analysis:
The `/InsecureDeserialization/task` endpoint in `InsecureDeserializationTask` class takes a Base64-encoded `token` parameter from the user and deserializes it:
```java
    b64token = token.replace('-', '+').replace('_', '/');

    try (ObjectInputStream ois =
        new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(Base64.getDecoder().decode(b64token)))) {
      before = System.currentTimeMillis();
      Object o = ois.readObject();
```
Deserialization is performed before checking the type of the restored object (`o instanceof VulnerableTaskHolder`), making the application vulnerable to Insecure Deserialization (CWE-502).
Additionally, the classpath contains `org.dummy.insecure.framework.VulnerableTaskHolder`, which implements a custom `readObject` method that directly runs system commands under certain conditions:
```java
  private void readObject(ObjectInputStream stream) throws Exception {
    stream.defaultReadObject();
    ...
    if ((taskAction.startsWith("sleep") || taskAction.startsWith("ping"))
        && taskAction.length() < 22) {
      log.info("about to execute: {}", taskAction);
      try {
        Process p = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(taskAction);
```
An attacker can serialize a `VulnerableTaskHolder` object with a malicious payload (e.g. `sleep 5`), encode it in Base64, and pass it as the `token` parameter. Upon deserialization, `VulnerableTaskHolder.readObject` will run the command, leading to Remote Code Execution / Command Injection.

Conceptual Exploit Payload:
Serialize a `VulnerableTaskHolder` instance:
`new VulnerableTaskHolder("test", "sleep 5")`
Encode the serialized byte array into Base64 and send it in a POST request to `/InsecureDeserialization/task`:
`token=[BASE64_SERIALIZED_OBJECT]`

