Analysis:
The `ProfileZipSlip` endpoint is vulnerable to the Zip Slip vulnerability, a form of path traversal via archive extraction.
The application accepts a `.zip` file from the user via the `uploadedFileZipSlip` multipart parameter.
In `processZipUpload`, the ZIP file contents are iterated through:
```java
      ZipFile zip = new ZipFile(uploadedZipFile.toFile());
      Enumeration<? extends ZipEntry> entries = zip.entries();
      while (entries.hasMoreElements()) {
        ZipEntry e = entries.nextElement();
        File f = new File(tmpZipDirectory.toFile(), e.getName());
        InputStream is = zip.getInputStream(e);
        Files.copy(is, f.toPath(), StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
      }
```
There is no validation on the name of each `ZipEntry` (`e.getName()`). If the ZIP file contains entry names with path traversal sequences (such as `../../target_file.png`), the file constructor `new File(tmpZipDirectory.toFile(), e.getName())` will resolve the file path outside of `tmpZipDirectory`.
When `Files.copy(is, f.toPath(), ...)` is called, it will write the extracted file outside of the temporary directory, allowing an attacker to write files to arbitrary directories (like `/PathTraversal/username`) and bypass constraints or overwrite profile pictures.

Conceptual Exploit Payload:
A ZIP archive containing a file entry named `../../test.jpg` or `../[username]/test.jpg`.
When POSTed to `/PathTraversal/zip-slip`, the ZIP extraction will resolve the path to the parent directory of `tmpZipDirectory` and write the file there.

